  "query_preview": "..\nMuch of the virga/light shower activity from this morning has \nmoved north/northeastward and/or dissipated this early\nafternoon. Showers have begun to build in the Huachuca \nMountains near Sierra Vista, with minimal activity elsewhere. \nThe latest HRRR run keeps the storm activity mainly areas south \nand east of Tucson, with the metro on the outer fringes of \nchances. With the dry low levels and DCAPE of 1400-1600 J/kg, \nthe greatest impacts with the storms continue to be the wind\ngusts of 40-60 mph and lofted areas of blowing dust around those\nstorms. Activity will decrease into the evening, with possibly\nsome lingering showers in Cochise County overnight.\n\nShower/thunderstorm activity tomorrow afternoon and evening\ncontinues to be focused on areas south and east of Tucson once\nagain with similar concerns of strong to severe outflow winds\nand areas of blowing dust. The HREF 24/12Z run has a decently\nsized area of 10% chance for 50 kts. This generally translates\nto a stronger wind day. \n\nOutside of the storm/shower chances, the hot temperatures\ncontinue this afternoon into tomorrow, with an Extreme Heat\nWarning in place. Moderate to Major HeatRisk will remain through\nFriday, with a decrease to Minor HeatRisk this weekend into\nearly next week as a dry low pressure system passes to the \nnorth of the area. This system will increase southwest winds as \nearly as Friday afternoon, with the stronger winds expected on \nSaturday and Sunday. The combination of the dry conditions and\nstrong winds this weekend leads to near to critical fire weather\nconditions each afternoon and evening, leading to the issuance\nof a Fire Weather Watch this weekend (more in the fire weather\nsection).\n\nOnce this low pressure system passes, the high pressure system\nto the east will slowly begin to move westward early to mid next\nweek, bringing back moisture and the potential for increased\nstorm activity.",
